ZEROAPP1 native guest application contract (original Zero implementation)

--app64 (mode8) and --app32 (mode9) compile hosted-style Zero source to
position-independent guest code with bounded memory streams. No Linux syscall
or firmware service is used by the application. These remain cooperative
shared-address-space kernel applications, not protected user processes.
Mode7 is reserved for the independently developed --windows target.

64-byte image header, u64 little-endian fields:
0 magic0x315050414f52455a (ZEROAPP1)
8 architecture64 or32
16 complete image size, including this header
24 entry offset64
32 minimum stack-region size4198400 (4MiB +4KiB)
40 ABI version1
48 reserved0
56 reserved0
The caller validates header/size/architecture, then calls image+64.
x64 uses the existing Zero Microsoft-style call ABI: RCX=context.
IA32 uses the existing Zero cdecl call ABI: [ESP+4]=context.

Context is caller-owned, zero initialized, at least192 bytes, all u64 fields:
0 reserved/version1 (caller sets1)
8 stackTop = stackBase+stackBytes
16 input address
24 input byte length
32 input consumed (initial0)
40 output address
48 output capacity
56 output produced (initial0)
64 argc (1 or2 for the Zero compiler)
72 argv0 pointer to NUL-terminated name
80 argv1 pointer to NUL-terminated target flag or0
88 returned low8-bit exit status
96 saved caller SP (application-private)
104..160 reserved/application-private
168 owned stackBase
176 owned stackBytes (>=4198400)
184 optional Zero filesystem service descriptor pointer, or0

The entry validates stack size, no wrap and exact stackTop, saves caller
callee-preserved registers and changes to the supplied stack. Its arena is
exactly4MiB and its64 local slots retain normal language value semantics.
The argc/argv memory at arenaBase+4194816 follows the existing Zero compiler's
own entry contract on both architectures. IA32 uses a separate internal frame
header above those argv records to avoid aliasing its arena cursor.

read copies up to the requested amount from the input stream and advances its
position. EOF returns0. write copies up to remaining output room and returns
the copied count; no remaining room returns unsigned negative ENOSPC(-28).
put appends one low byte or exits74 on full output. exit returns to the caller
with its low8 bits, also stored in context88. Falling off source returns0.
Memory streams need no service callback or external runtime.
Input/output address arithmetic is bounded; the caller must provide valid,
owned buffers and must not overlap the application image/stack/context.
There is no memory protection against explicitly unsafe Zero memory stores.

Supported operations: put/exit/read/write, memory load/store/reserve and
bitxor/bitand/shr. Firmware, port, foreign-call, fork/switch and positioned
file-descriptor operations are unavailable for these targets. The initial
compiler apps do not need them. Ezero inherits the supported memory-stream
capabilities. Whole-file services below access the selected guest volume.

The0.10 host interoperability extension also accepts host/argument/file API
source forms in guest targets, with explicit unavailable fallbacks: host,
argcount, argument, program and hostalloc return0; file operations,
hostprotect and hostfree return unsigned-1. No host filesystem is exposed.
Ezero host() consequently returns0; Ezero arguments remain unavailable.
Ezero file helpers use the whole-file services introduced below; they report
runtime errors when the service is absent or a filesystem operation fails.
The compiler's internal argc/argv frame above remains available for selecting
its output target; it is distinct from the guest host-argument API fallback.
See portability/host/ABI.ztext and ezero/HOST.ztext for the extension contract.

Whole-file service extension, revision1

The kernel may provide context184=S. S identifies its original filesystem
state machine through a private cooperative continuation. No shell command
is constructed or executed. Each request completes before the app resumes.
The descriptor and both continuations belong to the kernel; callers must
not modify these structures except through the compiler's builtin templates.
All descriptor fields are u64 little-endian:
  0 magic0x313053464f52455a (ZEROFS01)
  8 version1
 16 service continuation pointer (80 bytes)
 24 application continuation pointer (80 bytes)
 32 operation:1 readfile,2 save,3 writefile,4 create,5 del,
              6 watch,7 search,8 go,9 disks
 40 NUL-terminated path pointer, or0 for disks
 48 input/output buffer pointer
 56 input length or output capacity
 64 returned byte count,0,or unsigned-1
 72 filesystem driver result (fs/ABI.ztext)
 80 phase:1 request,2 completed
 88 reserved0
 96..255 kernel-private
The app checks magic/version/nonzero continuation pointers before publishing
a request. A missing or incompatible service returns unsigned-1.
The native continuation format for IA32 app frames is version2; the raw
kernel switch accepts this frame and restores the app arena and context.
Raw kernel/service frames retain continuation version1.

Builtins and successful results:
 :readfile[path|buffer|capacity] returns file bytes, without a trailing NUL.
 :save[path|buffer|length] creates or replaces and returns length.
 :writefile[path|buffer|length] creates exclusively and returns length.
 :create[path] returns0; an existing regular file is preserved.
 :del[path] returns0; a missing file is an error.
 :watch[root|buffer|capacity] returns NUL-separated filename bytes.
 :search[patternpath|buffer|capacity] returns the same list representation.
 :go[root] returns0 and changes the active terminal volume.
 :disks[buffer|capacity] returns NUL-separated logical volume roots.
Every failure returns unsigned-1. Lists count each filename terminator and
have no extra sentinel entry. An empty list returns0. Output is copied only
when the complete result fits, so capacity errors leave the caller buffer
unchanged. Input/output buffers must be valid owned memory, with capacities
at most65536. Shared-address-space apps remain trusted, not memory isolated.

Roots are A:| through Z:|. A relative name uses the current volume; a leading
| also selects its root. watch(.) and go(.) refer to the current root.
Only flat filenames of1..31 ASCII letters/digits/dot/underscore/hyphen are
supported; dot and dot-dot are not filenames. Search accepts * and ? in the
last component; plain names match exactly, an empty pattern matches all.
Writes accept at most400 printable ASCII bytes plus TAB, LF and CR. Profile validation happens
before mutation, and unsupported formats/features fail explicitly. ZeroFS
has eight file records. The existing restricted ext4/NTFS/Btrfs profiles and
durability rules also apply; this API does not extend their on-disk formats.
No formatting/partitioning service is exposed to applications.

Bundle/integration requirements
Reserve an8MiB kernel-owned workspace and load native application images for
the selected architecture: Zero compiler, Ezero frontend and Ezero packer.
Also provide the Ezero runtime prefix/body as source bytes. Reuse the same
application stack after each call returns. Buffers retain source/package/
generated Zero/output across calls. The kernel chooses --app64/--app32 via
context argv1 when invoking the Zero compiler.
The existing400-byte user-file limit can supply small source programs; larger
bundled examples/source buffers require an explicit separate command path.
Native binary persistence is not provided by this ABI.
UEFI can load bundle files and allocate workspace using existing boot services.
The BIOS path needs a safe loader-owned high-memory transfer, because the
conventional-memory kernel region cannot hold the compiler bundle as well.
